---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../consts';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Ali Alhejab is a Lead AI Engineer at HUMAIN in Riyadh, building agentic AI, real-time avatars, and generative image and video systems."
>
  <section class="page">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <p>
      I'm <strong>Ali Alhejab</strong>, a Lead AI Engineer at HUMAIN in {SITE.location}. I work
      across agentic AI, LLMs, image and video generation, deep learning, and computer vision.
    </p>

    <p>
      At HUMAIN, I built our in-house avatar models: a half-body model that runs in real time
      (25 fps, 720p) on a single NVIDIA L4, a face-inpainting model that serves 5+ concurrent
      streams per L4, and a compact avatar model optimized to run on-device on a laptop NPU. I
      develop real-time AI avatar agents for virtual presenting and meeting facilitation, with
      conversational pipelines that connect STT, LLM, and TTS, and I pretrain and fine-tune video
      diffusion models for custom avatars.
    </p>

    <h2>Background</h2>
    <p>
      Before HUMAIN, I was an AI Engineer at SDAIA, working across computer vision, NLP, and
      generative modeling. That included DETR and YOLO detection for construction-site
      surveillance, a sign-language-to-text system using T5 and AV-HuBERT, LoRA fine-tuning
      for Flux, Hunyuan, and Wan 2.1, and multi-GPU training with Hugging Face Accelerate and
      DeepSpeed. I also optimized vision and LLM inference with TensorRT, vLLM, and TeaCache.
    </p>

    <p>
      I came to AI from electrical engineering. During my MSc at KAUST, I took two courses that
      changed my direction: Mathematics for Machine Learning and Deep Learning for Visual
      Computing. Soon after, I mentored a group of students in KAUST's summer AI program, and
      that is where I fell in love with the field. Before KAUST, I earned a BSc in Electrical
      Engineering from KFUPM.
    </p>

    <h2>Research and writing</h2>
    <p>
      My work has been published with IEEE and Springer, spanning sign-language translation,
      photovoltaic fault localization, energy-efficient aerial IoT systems, and
      switched-capacitor converter design. I write here about the engineering side of AI:
      benchmarks, inference trade-offs, training pipelines, and the details that decide whether
      a system works outside a notebook.
    </p>

    <h2>How I work</h2>
    <p>
      I prefer small, testable steps over large unverified jumps. In practice that means
      reproducible experiments, clear baselines, and a healthy suspicion of a single benchmark.
      If a technique cannot be measured, it is hard to defend.
    </p>

    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      You can find me on <a href={SITE.github}>GitHub</a>,{' '}
      <a href={SITE.x}>X</a>, and{' '}
      <a href={SITE.scholar}>Google Scholar</a>, or reach me by{' '}
      <a href={`mailto:${SITE.email}`}>email</a>. The full CV, with
      publications, is <a href="/cv/">here</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
